From b609ffe7053a94ea50785705c56b96bf28b47ac5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 21:04:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] perf(parser): find a table row's pipes and escapes in one
 pass and decode from that record
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Splitting a GFM row into cells and decoding `\|` inside a cell asked the
same two questions of the same bytes: where the `|` bytes are, and whether
an odd run of backslashes sits in front of each one. The splitter answered
them with one `memchr` call per cell plus a backwards walk per pipe, and the
decoder then asked them again over every byte of every cell it was handed —
`escaped_pipe_scan_start` inspected each byte of a cell shorter than 64
bytes, which is nearly every cell a real table has.

`PipeCursor` in the new `parser::table_pipes` answers both once. One cursor
walks a row forward for the whole split. On aarch64 it classifies sixteen
bytes at a time with two `vceqq_u8` compares against `|` and `\`, narrowed
by the `vshrn_n_u16(_, 4)` movemask emulation the crate's other vector
scanners use, so each byte becomes one nibble of a `u64`. The escape state
of every pipe in a block then follows from that block's backslash mask by
bit arithmetic rather than a byte walk, and `memchr` still skips each
pipe-free stretch in one step so a sparse row is never scanned block by
block. Everywhere else the cursor keeps the original `memchr` probe and
backwards walk; that scalar engine is both the portable fallback and the
reference the vector path is tested against, over row shapes that place a
pipe at every offset around a block boundary behind backslash runs of every
parity, for every cursor start offset.

The escaped pipes the splitter passes over inside a cell are handed to the
decoder as an `EscapedPipes` record instead of being looked for again. An
empty record means the cell holds no `\|`, so it decodes to its borrowed
slice with no scan at all; a filled one bounds the decoder's walk to the
stretch between the first and the last escape.

Every row decodes the same because nothing about escape parity changed. A
backslash run is ended only by a non-backslash byte, and a cell begins
either at the row's content start or just past an unescaped `|`, with only
whitespace trimmed from it — so a run can never reach across a cell
boundary, and a pipe's parity is identical whether it is measured on the
row or on the cell's own trimmed slice. That is what makes the splitter's
row-coordinate record valid for the decoder after shifting it by the
trimmed cell's offset. Code spans, `\|`, leading and trailing pipes,
terminal pipe runs under `merged_table_cells`, malformed rows, alignment
rows and the cell source maps all take the same decisions as before.

Verified beyond the suite with a differential harness over ~8000 table
documents — fixed shapes plus three seeded random corpora with backslash
runs longer than a vector block — comparing the full pretty-printed AST
with spans and the rendered HTML with source spans, in both merged and
unmerged modes. The vector path and a build with the vector path forced off
both produce output byte-identical to the previous revision.

Ported unchanged from optimization round 4 (be641a8f) onto main after
#414: both cell splitters keep the byte-level whitespace helpers that
replaced `str::trim`, so a row's content bounds are found exactly as on
main.

