From d5f0dd31c54b73b5815369cbcd9170bd39b2d760 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 18:45:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] perf(parser): start table pipe windows at the pipe and
 stop the cell decoder at its last escape

Optimization round 4 measured the NEON pipe cursor at 1.29-1.43x on dense
escaped-pipe rows and 1.03-1.07x on plain rows, but 0.90-0.93x on
`table-formatted-*` (an escaped pipe every 26 bytes) and 0.96x on
`table-sparse-256`. Reading the cursor and the decoder's record handling
gives three reasons, and this change removes each one:

- A pipe outside the cached block cost more than the scalar scan paid.
  The scan asked `memchr` for it, as before, but then re-seated on the
  pipe's aligned block: a backwards walk to the block start and a full
  classification of that block, which a pipe with no neighbor in its block
  never repays. Every escaped pipe of a formatted cell paid this twice,
  once in the splitter and once in the decoder.
- The decoder was not bounded at the last escape. After its last `\|` it
  asked the cursor for one more pipe, which ran `memchr` to the next pipe
  or the end of the cell: the whole second half of a sparse cell.
- Every row paid setup even before its first pipe: construction classified
  the first block eagerly, and a row or tail shorter than a block was
  first copied into a zero-padded buffer. Header and delimiter rows are
  split twice each, and they are exactly these short rows.

The cursor now reads windows of sixteen bytes that start where the scan
is, not on aligned blocks, and nothing is read before the first pipe is
asked for. A spent window is followed by the next one inline; only a
window without a pipe hands the rest of the gap to `memchr`, and the next
window then starts on the pipe it found. Pipes closer than a window apart
therefore cost no call at all, and sparser ones at most the one call per
pipe the scalar scan made. A pipe is decided from the window's backslash
bits when a non-backslash byte precedes it in the window; otherwise (the
pipe opens the window, or only backslashes precede it there, at most once
per window) it takes the same bounded backwards walk as the scalar scan,
so the cursor carries no parity state between windows. The last window of
a row reloads the row's final sixteen bytes and shifts out the lanes
already read, as `line_scan` does, and a row shorter than a window is
classified by a byte loop.

The decoder runs its cursor on the cell cut right after the last recorded
escape, so it reads nothing past it. The merged-cell run scan no longer
needs a peek: a `|` directly after a pipe has no backslash in front of it,
so it is never escaped, and it is the next pipe the cursor reports.
`EscapedPipes` is a first/last pair with an empty sentinel, so recording
an escape is a `min` and a store.

Output stays byte-identical:

- Both masks are exact per byte (`vceqq_u8`), the shifted tail fills with
  zeros, and the short-row loop tests each byte. A pipe's escape state is
  the parity of the backslash run in front of it: when a non-backslash
  byte precedes it in the window the whole run is inside the window, and
  otherwise the reference walk decides.
- Every escaped pipe of a cell lies between the first and the last one the
  splitter recorded, because the splitter passes over every pipe inside a
  cell. A pipe's state depends only on the bytes before it, so cutting the
  cell after the last escape changes nothing up to the cut. Parity in row
  and in cell coordinates agrees, since a cell starts after a `|`,
  whitespace or the row start, and only whitespace is trimmed from it.
- The previous run scan's `!is_escaped_table_pipe(bytes, pipe + count)`
  was always true, because the byte before that position is a `|`.

The previous splitters and decoder are kept verbatim under `cfg(test)` as
oracles. New differential tests compare cells, offsets, spans, decoded
text, borrowing and every source-map boundary, in both splitting modes,
over ~95,000 systematic rows (a pipe at every offset 0-70 behind 0-20
backslashes inside eight row-edge shapes and eight suffixes), 30,000
seeded random rows mixing `|`, `\|`, `\\|`, backslash runs longer than a
window, code spans holding pipes, a backslash at the row end, leading and
trailing pipes and multibyte text, and every line of the frozen corpus
(`docs/reports/2026-09-14-simd-round/corpus.json.gz`), with and without
its line ending. The decoder is also compared on 4,000 standalone cells,
and the cursor against the scalar scan for every start offset and every
cut of each row shape. Outside the suite, an end-to-end harness built
against `main` and against this revision gave identical AST (with spans),
HTML, source-span HTML and arena bytes for 6,828 table-heavy documents
(the round-4 table diagnostics, 6,000 generated tables in block quotes,
lists and indentation with LF and CRLF endings, the GFM extension spec
examples and three frozen corpora) under six option sets, including
merged cells, table attributes, all extensions and MDX.

